; Supply-chain quarantine: never resolve a release younger than 30 days.
;
; The lesson-libs shelf (vendor/lesson-libs/README.md) has carried this rule as
; prose since #146 — "only take releases >= 30 days old" — which meant somebody
; comparing publish dates by hand. npm can enforce it: with `min-release-age`
; set, resolution refuses a version published inside the window and picks the
; newest one outside it instead. Age is the filter; the lockfile and the
; shelf's recorded sha256 remain the control.
;
; This binds `npm install`/`npm add`/`npm update` — the moment a new version
; actually enters the tree. It deliberately does NOT bind `npm ci`, which
; replays an already-reviewed lockfile; CI keeps installing exactly what is
; committed, at full speed.
;
; Needs npm >= 11.6, which is why package.json declares it under `engines`.
; An older npm prints `Unknown project config "min-release-age"` and installs
; anyway, so the setting is not self-enforcing. `engine-strict` is left off on
; purpose: it would fail `npm ci` on whatever npm a CI runner happens to ship,
; for a gate `npm ci` does not use. The place the gate must be proven live is
; the shelf refresh — vendor/lesson-libs/README.md step 1 checks it there,
; before resolving anything.
;
; No credentials belong in this file (AGENTS.md, public data boundary).
min-release-age=30
